Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $H,K$ be complex Hilbert spaces with the pairing linear in the first argument ([[def-real-and-complex-inner-product-space]], [[def-hilbert-space]]). Write $H\odot K$ for the complex vector space of [[def-tensor-product-of-modules-by-generators-and-relations]] applied to $R=\mathbb C$ and the $\mathbb C$-modules $H,K$: its underlying abelian group is the quotient $F/H_0$ of that definition, the class of $e_{(u,v)}$ is written $u\otimes v$, every element of $H\odot K$ is a finite sum of elementary tensors $u\otimes v$, and the defining relations make $(u,v)\mapsto u\otimes v$ $\mathbb C$-bilinear. Define, for finite sums,

$$\Bigl\langle\sum_i u_i\otimes v_i,\ \sum_j u'_j\otimes v'_j\Bigr\rangle:=\sum_{i,j}\langle u_i,u'_j\rangle_H\,\langle v_i,v'_j\rangle_K .$$

Then:

(a) *well-definedness and sesquilinearity*: the displayed value is independent of the representatives of its two arguments and defines a Hermitian sesquilinear form on $H\odot K$, linear in the first argument and conjugate-linear in the second;

(b) *positive definiteness and orthonormal coordinates*: $\langle z,z\rangle\ge0$ for every $z\in H\odot K$, with $\langle z,z\rangle=0$ exactly for $z=0$; more precisely, if $U_0\subseteq H$ and $V_0\subseteq K$ are finite-dimensional subspaces with orthonormal bases $e_1,\dots,e_p$ and $f_1,\dots,f_q$, then every $z$ in the span of the elementary tensors from $U_0\times V_0$ has a unique expansion $z=\sum_{a,b}c_{ab}\,e_a\otimes f_b$ and $\langle z,z\rangle=\sum_{a,b}|c_{ab}|^2$; in particular the assignment is an inner product on $H\odot K$;

(c) *completion*: the norm completion $(\widehat{H\odot K},\kappa)$ of $H\odot K$ ([[def-completion-of-a-normed-space]]) exists and, with the unique inner product extending the given one, is a complex Hilbert space; $\kappa$ is a linear isometry with dense image, $\|\kappa(u\otimes v)\|=\|u\|\,\|v\|$, and any two completions of $H\odot K$ are identified by a unique surjective linear isometry fixing $H\odot K$ pointwise;

(d) *product bases*: if $(e_a)_{a\in A}$ and $(f_b)_{b\in B}$ are orthonormal families in $H$ and $K$ whose closed linear spans are $H$ and $K$, then $(\kappa(e_a\otimes f_b))_{(a,b)\in A\times B}$ is an orthonormal family in $\widehat{H\odot K}$ whose closed linear span is $\widehat{H\odot K}$; if $A$ and $B$ are countable then $\widehat{H\odot K}$ is separable.

The notation $H\widehat{\otimes}K:=\widehat{H\odot K}$ is used in the rest of this pair. No assertion is made about unbounded operators or algebraic sums of non-completed tensor products.

Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $H$ be a complex Hilbert space with the pairing linear in the first argument ([[def-hilbert-space]], [[def-real-and-complex-inner-product-space]]), and for $u,v\in H$ let

$$T_{u,v}x:=\langle x,v\rangle u\qquad(x\in H).$$

Then:

(a) $T_{u,v}\in\mathcal B(H)$ with $\|T_{u,v}\|=\|u\|\,\|v\|$ ([[def-bounded-linear-operator]], [[def-operator-norm]]), and the assignment $(u,v)\mapsto T_{u,v}$ is linear in $u$ and conjugate-linear in $v$;

(b) $T_{u,v}$ is trace class ([[def-trace-class-operator]]) with $\|T_{u,v}\|_1=\|u\|\,\|v\|$ and $\operatorname{tr}(T_{u,v})=\langle u,v\rangle$;

(c) $T_{u,v}^*=T_{v,u}$ ([[def-hilbert-space-adjoint]]); $T_{x,x}\ge0$ with $\operatorname{tr}(T_{x,x})=\|x\|^2$ and $\|T_{x,x}\|_1=\|x\|^2$ ([[def-non-negative-and-positive-operator]]);

(d) for bounded $B\in\mathcal B(H)$, $BT_{u,v}=T_{Bu,v}$ and $T_{u,v}B=T_{u,B^*v}$;

(e) for trace-class $S$, $ST_{u,v}=T_{Su,v}$, $T_{u,v}S=T_{u,S^*v}$ and $\operatorname{tr}(ST_{x,y})=\langle Sx,y\rangle=\operatorname{tr}(T_{x,y}S)$;

(f) the map $(u,v)\mapsto T_{u,v}$ is continuous $H\times H\to\mathcal S_1(H)$ for the trace norm, so in particular $v\mapsto T_{v,v}$ and $v\mapsto T_{u,v}$ are trace-norm continuous for fixed $u$.

*Scope.* Only the rank-one calculus is asserted; no spectral, compactness or ideal statement beyond (b) is claimed, and no physical interpretation is attached.
